## 풀이
BFS를 통해 지형을 탐색하면서 통나무가 도착지점에 다다르는 순간 탐색을 멈추고 이동 횟수를 반환하였습니다!

With [C# 11](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/tutorials/static-virtual-interface-members) it became possible to define static (property) members. This allows code like:

``` C#
public interface IEmpty<TSelf> where TSelf : struct, IEmpty<TSelf>
{
    /// <summary>Represents an empty/not set <typeparamref name="TSelf"/>.</summary>
    static abstract TSelf Empty { get; }
}
```

As a result, it is to be expected that SVO's with a well defined empty state not only use to do so via static read-only fields, but properties as well. Hence this PR.

This adds support for offset pagination. The same `Page` and `Pageable` abstractions are used, with a mild adjustment to the `loadPage` and `hasNextPage` helper functions.

With offset pagination, we define the stopping condition (via `hasNextPage`) simply when the server responds with no remaining elements.
* There isn't a "next" response property for us to act upon like in cursor pagination, so this is the best we can do (and is consistent with the other SDKs).
* The generated code is equivalent to the cursor pagination strategy excluding the final `core.Pageable` instantiation shown below:

```typescript
let _offset = request.page != null ? request.page : 1;
return new core.Pageable<SeedPagination.ListUsersPaginationResponse, SeedPagination.User>({
    response: await list(request),
    hasNextPage: (response) => (response?.data ?? []).length > 0,
    getItems: (response) => response?.data ?? [],
    loadPage: (_response) => {
        _offset += 1;
        return list({
            ...request,
            page: _offset,
        });
    },
});
```
